{"subjectId":"java","id":"stringbuilder","title":"StringBuilder & StringBuffer","group":"Core Language: Strings, Arrays & Methods","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A <em>mutable</em> character buffer. Use <code>StringBuilder</code> whenever you build a string piece by piece — especially in loops.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>String</code></th><th><code>StringBuilder</code></th><th><code>StringBuffer</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mutable?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Thread-safe?</td><td>Yes (immutable)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (<code>synchronized</code> methods)</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slow for repeated edits</td><td><strong>Fastest</strong></td><td>Slower (locking)</td></tr><tr><td>Since</td><td>1.0</td><td>1.5</td><td>1.0</td></tr><tr><td>Use when</td><td>Values that don't change</td><td>Building strings in one thread (almost always)</td><td>Legacy code; shared buffer across threads (rare)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tool\"></i>Core operations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"Java\");\n        sb.append(' ').append(21).append(\"!\");     // chaining — each call returns this\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        sb.insert(0, \"&gt;&gt; \");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        sb.replace(3, 7, \"JAVA\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        sb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1).delete(0, 3);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        sb.setCharAt(0, 'j');\n        System.out.println(sb.reverse());\n        System.out.println(sb.length() + \" \" + sb.indexOf(\"AV\") + \" \" + sb.charAt(0));\n        String result = sb.toString();              // freeze into an immutable String\n        System.out.println(result);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Java 21!\n&gt;&gt; Java 21!\n&gt;&gt; JAVA 21!\nJAVA 21\n12 AVAj\n7 3 1\n12 AVAj</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Capacity</h3>\n<p>A <code>StringBuilder</code> has a <em>capacity</em> (default 16). When full, it grows to roughly <code>(old * 2) + 2</code> and copies — amortised O(1) per append. Pre-size with <code>new StringBuilder(expectedLength)</code> when you know it.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        System.out.println(sb.capacity());\n        sb.append(\"12345678901234567\");            // 17 chars &gt; 16\n        System.out.println(sb.capacity());\n        System.out.println(new StringBuilder(\"abc\").capacity());   // 16 + 3\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>16\n34\n19</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Pitfalls</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        StringBuilder a = new StringBuilder(\"x\");\n        StringBuilder b = new StringBuilder(\"x\");\n        System.out.println(a.equals(b));                          // identity! not overridden\n        System.out.println(a.toString().equals(b.toString()));\n        System.out.println(a.compareTo(b) == 0);                  // Java 11+: content compare\n\n        StringBuilder c = new StringBuilder('A');                  // char → int capacity 65!\n        System.out.println(\"[\" + c + \"] \" + c.capacity());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>false\ntrue\ntrue\n[] 65</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>StringBuilder</code> does <strong>not</strong> override <code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code> — compare <code>toString()</code> values (or use <code>compareTo</code>, Java 11+). And <code>new StringBuilder('A')</code> silently calls the <code>int capacity</code> constructor.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">When does + matter?</div><p>The compiler already turns <code>a + b + c</code> in a <em>single expression</em> into efficient code. The problem is <code>+=</code> inside a loop: each iteration creates a new <code>String</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is StringBuilder faster than String concatenation in a loop?</summary><div><p><code>String</code> is immutable, so each <code>s += x</code> allocates a new string and copies all previous characters: O(n²) total. <code>StringBuilder</code> appends into one resizable array: O(n) amortised.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>StringBuilder vs StringBuffer?</summary><div><p>Same API. <code>StringBuffer</code> methods are <code>synchronized</code> (thread-safe, slower); <code>StringBuilder</code> is not synchronized and is the default choice. Sharing a builder between threads is a design smell anyway.</p></div></details></section>"}